new = '''**Zero stability under escalating attack (Z(\u03b1)).** Tracking the cumulative ASR round by round exposes distinct stability morphologies (Figure: zcurve7). D2 holds near 0.2 through round 1 and then climbs monotonically to **0.833 by round 7** (each rewrite learns another way around the keyword vocabulary; the 3-round estimate of 0.467 understates the true collapse). D4 jumps at round 2 and plateaus (0.067 \u2192 0.300 \u2192 0.300: rewrites learn the schema-legal forms quickly, then exhaust the space). D1 and D3 stay at 0.000 across all seven rounds. The two flat curves are indistinguishable in Z(\u03b1) alone\u2014which is exactly the point. **Identical observed zero-stability profiles do not, by themselves, establish equivalent security guarantees**; the outcome (zero stability) is the same, and only the provenance separates the guarantees. The gap widens under escalation: under 7-round adaptive attacks, D3's model is driven to propose the malicious tool in **83.3%** of cases and is still blocked every time (the gate reads a platform record under an uncompromised authorization/audit substrate), while D1's model is driven 36.7% and the zero is its refusal behavior holding. We attempted to falsify D1's flat curve directly (escalating 7-round adaptive attacks with attacker prompts explicitly demanding complete executable arguments, plus authority/emergency/role-play frames): it did not break\u2014the behavioral zero is stable under this attack budget\u2014which we read as stronger support for the provenance distinction, not as evidence that it cannot break. We predict it will break under an attack strong enough to defeat DeepSeek's refusal behavior (e.g., a less-aligned attacker model), and D3's will not, short of compromising the audit substrate itself. This is a proof-of-concept characterization at seven discrete attack stages (30 cases per stage), not a high-resolution estimate of a continuous failure frontier; the gradient that could falsify the prediction is left to future work.'''

